\name{terms_mrqap}
\alias{terms_mrqap}
\title{Terms of an MRQAP formula}
\description{
The terms that the right-hand side of a formula for \code{\link[=estimate_mrqap]{estimate_mrqap()}} can
name, what each one measures, and the equation of the matrix it builds.
Each term builds one or more columns of the model, each a matrix with one
cell for each dyad of the network.
Every term is named here as it is written in a formula, such as \code{same(x)};
the name of its coefficient is the name of its column, such as \code{same_dept}.

\code{specify_mrqap(.data = net)} lists the terms that suit a network, written
out for its layers and node attributes (see \link{specify_terms}).
A formula written for \code{{ergm}} or \code{{goldfish}} names the terms in their
own way; the last section gives each term's equivalent there, and
\code{\link[=specify_mrqap]{specify_mrqap()}} rewrites a formula from one model for another.
}
\section{Notation}{

A cell \eqn{x_{ij}} of a term's matrix is for the dyad of sender (ego)
\eqn{i} and receiver (alter) \eqn{j}. In a two-mode network, \eqn{i} is a
node of the first mode and \eqn{j} of the second.
\eqn{a_i} is node \eqn{i}'s value of the attribute \code{x},
\eqn{t_{ij}} the value of the tie from \eqn{i} to \eqn{j} in the layer
\code{layer}, and \eqn{y_{ij}} the value of the tie in the outcome.
\eqn{\mathbf{1}[\cdot]}{1[.]} is 1 where the condition holds and 0
otherwise. In a one-mode network, the diagonal \eqn{i = j} is not a
dyad, and is left out of the model.
}

\section{Terms of node attributes}{

\itemize{
\item \code{ego(x, levels = NULL)} is the sender's value,
\deqn{x_{ij} = a_i.}{x_ij = a_i.}
Its column is \code{ego_x}.
A categorical attribute gives one 0/1 column for each category
\eqn{c}, \code{ego_x_c}, with \eqn{x_{ij} = \mathbf{1}[a_i = c]}{x_ij = 1[a_i = c]},
less the first category as the reference. \verb{levels =} chooses the
categories that get a column; the categories it leaves out are then
the reference together.
\item \code{alter(x, levels = NULL)} is the receiver's value,
\eqn{x_{ij} = a_j}{x_ij = a_j}, as \code{alter_x}, and is dummy-coded as
\code{ego()} is.
\item \code{same(x, diff = FALSE, levels = NULL)} is 1 where the sender and the
receiver share a category,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \mathbf{1}[a_i = a_j],}{x_ij = 1[a_i = a_j],}
as \code{same_x}. With \verb{levels =}, only a category in \eqn{L} counts:
\eqn{x_{ij} = \mathbf{1}[a_i = a_j \in L]}{x_ij = 1[a_i = a_j in L]}.
With \code{diff = TRUE}, as in ergm's \code{nodematch()}, it gives one column for
each category, \code{same_x_c}, with
\eqn{x_{ij} = \mathbf{1}[a_i = a_j = c]}{x_ij = 1[a_i = a_j = c]};
a dyad of two different categories is in none of them, and is the
reference.
In a two-mode network, no tie joins two nodes of one mode, so \code{same()}
is the share of the sender's other ties in the outcome that go to
nodes like the receiver, where the attribute is only on the second
mode,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \frac{\sum_{k \neq j} y_{ik} \mathbf{1}[a_k = a_j]}{\sum_{k \neq j} y_{ik}},}{x_ij = sum_(k != j) y_ik 1[a_k = a_j] / sum_(k != j) y_ik,}
and otherwise the share of the receiver's other ties that come from
nodes like the sender,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \frac{\sum_{k \neq i} y_{kj} \mathbf{1}[a_k = a_i]}{\sum_{k \neq i} y_{kj}},}{x_ij = sum_(k != i) y_kj 1[a_k = a_i] / sum_(k != i) y_kj,}
or 0 where there is no other tie. This reads the outcome's ties.
\item \code{mix(x, levels = NULL)} gives one 0/1 column for each pair of a
sender's category \eqn{c} and a receiver's category \eqn{d},
\code{mix_x_c_d}, with
\deqn{x_{ij} = \mathbf{1}[a_i = c,\ a_j = d],}{x_ij = 1[a_i = c, a_j = d],}
less the first pair as the reference. In an undirected network the pair
is unordered, and \eqn{x_{ij}} is also 1 where \eqn{a_i = d} and
\eqn{a_j = c}. The pairs are in ergm's order for \code{nodemix()}, the
sender's category changing fastest, and \verb{levels =} names a pair as
\code{"c -> d"}, or \code{"c -- d"} where it is unordered.
\item \code{diff(x, transformer_fn = abs)} is the transformed difference between
the sender's and the receiver's values,
\deqn{x_{ij} = f(a_i - a_j),}{x_ij = f(a_i - a_j),}
as \code{diff_x}: the absolute difference \eqn{|a_i - a_j|} by default, and
the signed difference with \code{transformer_fn = identity}.
\item \code{sim(x)} is the similarity of the two values, as a share of the largest
difference between any sender and receiver,
\deqn{x_{ij} = 1 - \frac{|a_i - a_j|}{\max_{kl} |a_k - a_l|},}{x_ij = 1 - |a_i - a_j| / max_kl |a_k - a_l|,}
as \code{sim_x}. In a one-mode network, the largest difference is the range
of the attribute.
}

\code{same()}, \code{same(diff = TRUE)}, and \code{mix()} of one attribute measure its
homophily in different ways, as \code{diff()} and \code{sim()} do, so a model takes
one of each. \code{diff()} and \code{sim()} need a numeric attribute.
\code{match(x)} is a deprecated alias of \code{same(x, diff = TRUE)}, and \code{dist(x)}
of \code{diff(x)}.
}

\section{Terms of other layers}{

These read another layer of a multiplex network, or a tie attribute, and
refuse the outcome's own layer, since they would predict the outcome's
ties from those same ties.
\itemize{
\item \code{tie(layer, weighted = FALSE)} is 1 where the layer has a tie,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \mathbf{1}[t_{ij} > 0],}{x_ij = 1[t_ij > 0],}
or its value, \eqn{x_{ij} = t_{ij}}{x_ij = t_ij}, with
\code{weighted = TRUE}, as \code{tie_layer}. A layer named on its own enters with
its values as stored, as \code{layer}.
\item \code{exchange(layer, weighted = NULL)} is the reverse tie,
\deqn{x_{ij} = t_{ji},}{x_ij = t_ji,}
as \code{exchange_layer}, with its values as stored;
\eqn{\mathbf{1}[t_{ji} > 0]}{1[t_ji > 0]} with \code{weighted = FALSE}.
\code{recip(layer)} is the same, as \code{recip_layer}.
\item \code{indeg(layer, node = "alter", weighted = FALSE)} is the receiver's
number of incoming ties, \eqn{x_{ij} = \sum_{k} w_{kj}}{x_ij = sum_k w_kj},
as \code{indeg_layer}, or the sender's with \code{node = "ego"},
\eqn{x_{ij} = \sum_{k} w_{ki}}{x_ij = sum_k w_ki}, as \code{ego_indeg_layer}.
\item \code{outdeg(layer, node = "ego", weighted = FALSE)} is the sender's number
of outgoing ties, \eqn{x_{ij} = \sum_{k} w_{ik}}{x_ij = sum_k w_ik}, as
\code{ego_outdeg_layer}, or the receiver's with \code{node = "alter"},
\eqn{x_{ij} = \sum_{k} w_{jk}}{x_ij = sum_k w_jk}, as \code{outdeg_layer}.
}

In the degrees, \eqn{w_{kl} = \mathbf{1}[t_{kl} > 0]}{w_kl = 1[t_kl > 0]},
or \eqn{t_{kl}} with \code{weighted = TRUE}; a loop \eqn{k = l} and an
unobserved tie are not counted. In a two-mode network only the second
mode receives and only the first sends, so \code{indeg(node = "ego")} and
\code{outdeg(node = "alter")} abort. \verb{type =} is a deprecated alias of
\verb{node =}, and goldfish's name for it. goldfish reads the receiver for
both, so a formula written for goldfish is read as goldfish reads it, and
goldfish's \code{indeg} or \code{outdeg} on its own, the outcome's own degree, is
dropped.
}

\section{Terms of others' attributes}{

\itemize{
\item \code{tertius(layer, x, summarizer_fn = "mean")} is the mean value of the
receiver's other in-neighbours in the layer: the nodes \eqn{k},
other than the sender, with a tie to the receiver,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \frac{1}{|N_{ij}|} \sum_{k \in N_{ij}} a_k, \quad N_{ij} = \{k \neq i : t_{kj} > 0\},}{x_ij = mean of a_k over N_ij = {k != i : t_kj > 0},}
as \code{tertius_layer_x} (Haunss and Hollway 2023). A node \eqn{k} with
no value is left out of \eqn{N_{ij}}, and where \eqn{N_{ij}} is empty,
\eqn{x_{ij}} is the mean of the other cells. With
\code{summarizer_fn = "sum"}, it is the sum weighted by the tie's value,
\eqn{x_{ij} = \sum_{k \neq i} t_{kj} a_k}{x_ij = sum_(k != i) t_kj a_k}.
\item \code{tertius_diff(layer, x, summarizer_fn = "mean")} is how far the
sender's value lies from that mean,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \left|a_i - \frac{1}{|N_{ij}|} \sum_{k \in N_{ij}} a_k\right|,}{x_ij = |a_i - mean of a_k over N_ij|,}
as \code{tertius_diff_layer_x}, and is filled in the same way.
}

Both need a numeric attribute. \code{tertius(x)} without a layer reads the
outcome's own ties, and warns.
}

\section{Terms of the outcome's previous wave}{

On a longitudinal network, these read the outcome in the wave before,
\eqn{y^{(w-1)}}, as goldfish and RSiena read the network before each
change. On a network of one wave, they could only read the outcome's own
ties, so \code{inertia} and \code{trans} are dropped, and \code{recip} warns.
\itemize{
\item \code{inertia} is 1 where the tie was there in the wave before,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \mathbf{1}[y^{(w-1)}_{ij} > 0],}{x_ij = 1[y(w-1)_ij > 0],}
or its value with \code{inertia(weighted = TRUE)}.
\item \code{recip} is the reverse tie in the wave before,
\eqn{x_{ij} = y^{(w-1)}_{ji}}{x_ij = y(w-1)_ji}.
\item \code{trans} is the number of two-paths from the sender to the receiver in
the wave before,
\deqn{x_{ij} = \sum_{k} b_{ik} b_{kj}, \quad b_{kl} = \mathbf{1}[y^{(w-1)}_{kl} > 0],}{x_ij = sum_k b_ik b_kj, b_kl = 1[y(w-1)_kl > 0],}
where an unobserved tie counts as 0.
}
}

\section{Terms of a list of networks}{

\itemize{
\item \code{global(x)} is a variable \eqn{g_m} of the whole network \eqn{m}, from
its \code{globals}, for every dyad of that network:
\eqn{x_{ij} = g_m}{x_ij = g_m}, as \code{global_x}. On one network it is the
intercept, so it needs a list of networks.
}
}

\section{Terms of the reporter of a cognitive social structure}{

A cognitive social structure (CSS) holds each reporter \eqn{r}'s report
of the network, so each cell is \eqn{x_{ijr}}, for the dyad as \eqn{r}
reports it (Krackhardt 1987).
\itemize{
\item \code{reporter(x, levels = NULL)} is the reporter's value,
\eqn{x_{ijr} = a_r}{x_ijr = a_r}, as \code{reporter_x}, and is dummy-coded
as \code{ego()} is.
\item \code{reporter_ego} is 1 where the reporter is the sender,
\eqn{x_{ijr} = \mathbf{1}[r = i]}{x_ijr = 1[r = i]}, and
\code{reporter_alter} where the reporter is the receiver,
\eqn{x_{ijr} = \mathbf{1}[r = j]}{x_ijr = 1[r = j]}: a report of the
reporter's own ties.
\item \code{reporter_same(x, node = "ego")} is 1 where the reporter shares the
sender's category, \eqn{x_{ijr} = \mathbf{1}[a_r = a_i]}{x_ijr = 1[a_r = a_i]},
as \code{reporter_same_ego_x}, or the receiver's with \code{node = "alter"},
\eqn{x_{ijr} = \mathbf{1}[a_r = a_j]}{x_ijr = 1[a_r = a_j]}.
}

See the section on cognitive social structures in \code{\link[=estimate_mrqap]{estimate_mrqap()}}.
}

\section{Choosing categories with levels =}{

\verb{levels =} chooses the categories that get a column in \code{ego()},
\code{alter()}, and \code{same()}, or the pairs in \code{mix()}, as in ergm. Give the
names, such as \code{levels = c("sales", "hr")} or, for \code{mix()},
\code{levels = "sales -> hr"}, written as in \code{fit$terms} (\verb{--} for an
undirected network). Or give positions in the order of the columns,
where a negative position leaves that one out: \code{levels = -1} is the
default of \code{ego()}, \code{alter()}, and \code{mix()}. In \code{ego()}, \code{alter()}, and
\code{mix()}, the categories left out are the reference together; where every
one is chosen, the first chosen is the reference. In \code{same()}, a dyad
that shares a category left out is not counted. An unknown name aborts
and lists the categories. A term with more than ten categories and no
\verb{levels =} warns, since under \code{permute = "predictor"} each column is
permuted on its own.
}

\section{Interactions}{

\code{:} and \code{*} give interactions of any order. An interaction is the
product of its terms' cells, \eqn{x_{ij} = x^{(1)}_{ij} x^{(2)}_{ij}}{x_ij = x1_ij x2_ij},
with one column for each pair of their columns, named by joining their
names with \code{:}, such as \code{ego_age:same_office}.
}

\section{Equivalents in other models}{

A formula written for \code{{ergm}} or \code{{goldfish}} can be used as it is, and
\code{\link[=specify_mrqap]{specify_mrqap()}} and \code{\link[=specify_ergm]{specify_ergm()}} rewrite a formula for each other's
model. The nearest term of each is:\tabular{llll}{
   infernet \tab ergm \tab goldfish \tab RSiena \cr
   \code{ego(x)} \tab \code{nodeocov("x")}, \code{nodeofactor("x")}; \code{b1cov("x")} in a two-mode network; \code{nodecov("x")} in an undirected one \tab \code{ego(x)} \tab \code{egoX} \cr
   \code{alter(x)} \tab \code{nodeicov("x")}, \code{nodeifactor("x")}; \code{b2cov("x")} in a two-mode network \tab \code{alter(x)} \tab \code{altX} \cr
   \code{same(x)} \tab \code{nodematch("x")} \tab \code{same(x)} \tab \code{sameX} \cr
   \code{same(x, diff = TRUE)} \tab \code{nodematch("x", diff = TRUE)} \tab  \tab  \cr
   \code{mix(x)} \tab \code{nodemix("x")} \tab  \tab  \cr
   \code{ego(x, levels = )}, \code{same(x, levels = )} \tab \code{nodeofactor("x", levels = )}, \code{nodematch("x", levels = )} \tab  \tab  \cr
   \code{mix(x, levels = )} \tab \code{nodemix("x", levels2 = )} \tab  \tab  \cr
   \code{diff(x)} \tab \code{absdiff("x")} \tab \code{diff(x)} \tab \code{absDiffX} \cr
   \code{diff(x, transformer_fn = identity)} \tab \code{diff("x")} \tab \code{diff(x, transformer_fn = identity)} \tab \code{diffX} \cr
   \code{sim(x)} \tab \code{edgecov()} \tab \code{sim(x)}, as minus the absolute difference \tab \code{simX}, centred \cr
   a layer, or \code{tie(layer)} \tab \code{edgecov()} \tab \code{tie(layer)} \tab \code{X} \cr
   \code{exchange(layer)} \tab \code{edgecov()} of the transpose \tab \code{recip(layer)} \tab  \cr
   \code{indeg(layer)}, \code{outdeg(layer)} \tab \code{edgecov()} \tab \code{indeg(layer)}, \code{outdeg(layer, type = "ego")} \tab  \cr
   \code{tertius(layer, x)}, \code{tertius_diff(layer, x)} \tab \code{edgecov()} \tab the same, with the sender among the in-neighbours \tab  \cr
   \code{global(x)} \tab  \tab \code{global(x)} \tab  \cr
   \code{recip} \tab \code{mutual} \tab \code{recip} \tab \code{recip} \cr
   \code{inertia} \tab  \tab \code{inertia} \tab  \cr
   \code{trans} \tab \code{ttriple} \tab \code{trans} \tab \code{transTrip} \cr
}


\code{sim()} is on another scale in goldfish, which changes its coefficient
but not its test, and goldfish's \code{tertius()} counts the sender among the
in-neighbours, so \code{\link[=specify_mrqap]{specify_mrqap()}} names both where it reads a goldfish
formula. ergm's \code{edges} is the intercept, which an MRQAP always has.
The reporter terms have no equivalent, since no other model has a
reporter. ergm's terms of the outcome's own structure, such as \code{gwesp()},
and goldfish's endogenous effects, such as \code{cycle}, have no MRQAP term,
and an MRQAP drops them (see \link{specify}).
}

